On the repository that produced this post, the GitHub Actions job that verifies
an integrated candidate spends 5 seconds starting up. It spends another 14
seconds installing dependencies and 17 seconds building workspace packages, and
only then can it run a single check. Those are medians across 18 successful
runs. On the machine that wrote the code, where the install and the build are
already sitting on disk, the checks take 4.5 seconds at the median.

Setup outweighs work there by about eight to one. That is the economic argument
for moving verification to the edge, and it raises the question this note is
about. What can a merge gate require, once the suites have already run somewhere
it does not control?

We took a position and then measured a corpus we own, to see whether it held.
That corpus is small and young. Where that limits the claims we say so, and one
section covers the night the whole thing caught us out.

## The workload changed shape

The [Octoverse 2025 report](https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/)
counts nearly a billion commits pushed to GitHub in 2025, up 25.1% year over
year. Underneath that total, agents now write in overlapping bursts on the same
repository, and that is what changes the job of a merge gate.

[Xu, Subramanian and Karthik](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697v2) studied 33,596
agent-authored pull requests across 2,807 repositories. They found that 79.4% of
them overlapped in time with another agent-authored pull request on the same
repository, and inside a one-week window that rises to 95.0%. When the two pull
requests came from different agents, 41.7% of the pairs conflicted textually,
against 19.8% for pairs from one agent. Of the conflicted files, 84.4% were
source rather than dependency manifests.

So the branch a suite passed on is often a different tree from the one that
lands. [Sakib, Banik and Jadliwala](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.12428v1) audited
agent-authored pull requests for security code smells and found at least one in
38.9% of them. They also report that 81.1% of the genuine secret leaks in their
corpus reached merge undetected. Given those numbers, we were never going to
answer the cost problem by loosening the gate.

Our own repository sits inside this workload. Between 28 July and 3 August 2026,
agents wrote 524 commits across 131 merged pull requests, over 10 packages and 2
apps carrying 1,928 tests.

## What the literature attests

A 2026 survey led by [Yiqi Wang](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04990) defines
execution provenance as the typed graph of an agent execution, and evidence
tracing as that graph projected onto evidence-support relations. Trust functions
apply to the graph. That framing is coherent, and most of the field is working
inside it.

Rashidi's [systematization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05743) collects
39 papers published between 2023 and 2026 into 17 categories: sandbox
isolation, capability and access control, policy enforcement, time-of-check to
time-of-use races, Model Context Protocol threats, identity delegation,
execution provenance, network egress control, static analysis of agent output.
Every one of them works on the agent itself, watching it run or limiting what it
can reach.

One number in that paper stuck with us. Policy-enforcement studies in its corpus
show failure rates between 69% and 98% of real denylists, and the isolation
literature does not address them. Rashidi names four other cross-cutting gaps.
Nobody studies policy-authoring errors, and benign out-of-scope agent actions
occur in up to 17.1% of cases with no coverage in the access-control work.

## The position

thwip attests the fixed point of the execution: the tree the run leaves behind.

A signed trace of an agent session makes a claim nobody can test at merge time.
You cannot replay the session, so the trace is worth exactly the integrity of
whatever recorded it. A git tree is a fixed object anybody can pull down and
re-run the suites against, and the claim attached to it either reproduces or it
does not.

So thwip throws the trace away. The in-toto subject is a git tree:

```json
"subject": [{ "name": "git-tree", "digest": { "sha1": "…" } }]
```

`packages/protocol/src/schema.ts` refuses any other evaluation mode, with a
comment that says why:

```ts
// A proof covers the head tree. A proof claiming to cover anything else is
// a proof this schema has no rules for, so it is not a proof.
const evaluationMode = git["evaluationMode"];
if (evaluationMode !== "head-tree") return fail("git.evaluationMode invalid");
```

That puts the agent outside the boundary where pass and fail get decided. An
agent can drive the CLI, write the commit message and open the pull request. An
enrolled device key does the signing, and the GitHub App evaluates the result.
The decision reads none of the agent's runtime behaviour, so constraining that
behaviour would add nothing to it.

The time-of-check to time-of-use category has no window to attack here, because
the object under check is an immutable content hash. The whole class of
state-validation races collapses into one reason code,
`SUBJECT_TREE_MISMATCH`, which fires when the tree the proof covers differs from
the tree GitHub is asked to merge.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-trace-vs-tree.svg" alt="An agent execution graph of tool calls and file writes, reduced to a single tree hash" caption="Figure 1. The typed execution graph the survey literature attests, reduced to the one object thwip signs. We throw away everything to the left of the hash on purpose." /%}

## What is implemented

Evidence rides the commit as a git trailer, `thwip-proof: v1.<base64url DSSE
envelope>`, wrapping an [in-toto](https://in-toto.io/) Statement v1 with
predicate `urn:thwip:attestation:ci-run:v1`. The envelope is
[DSSE](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/dsse), signed by an OpenSSH ed25519
key that the signer has enrolled on GitHub as a **Signing** key. GitHub's
Authentication keys do not count for this, and a human has to register the
device.

The predicate carries the tree OID and its parents, the base ref and base sha,
a policy digest and version, a pipeline digest and runner, the suite results
with durations, and the signer's key id and GitHub user id. It also carries
`networkMode` and `cacheMode`. Those two are things the signer declares about
its own posture. Nothing observes them, the verifier treats them as
declarations, and we write that down here so nobody reads them as verified
facts.

The thwip GitHub App owns the required `thwip` check and
[executes no repository code](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-app-native-proof-checks.md).
It reads GitHub for permission and key enrollment, fetches a signed fact
bundle, and fails closed across a fixed vocabulary of 29 reason codes. One of
those codes says only that the evidence has not settled yet. A handful of the
other 28: `SUBJECT_TREE_MISMATCH`, `SUBJECT_BASE_MISMATCH`,
`SIGNER_KEY_REVOKED`, `SIGNER_PERMISSION_INSUFFICIENT`,
`PIPELINE_DIGEST_MISMATCH`, `SUITE_NOT_PASSED`, `PROOF_EXPIRED`.

The concurrency finding above is why a head proof cannot stand on its own. A
merge queue holds the lane and records every transition as a signed record.
There are seven kinds of record, and each claims something specific. If the
queue verified an integrated candidate, the record for that merge cites the
verifier's result. Nothing gets cited when a merge goes through on the head's
own proof, because no verifier ran. `GET /v1/queue/records/:digest` returns the
exact bytes that were signed.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-toctou.svg" alt="Timeline showing a validation-to-use window in the observe model, and no window when the subject is a content hash" caption="Figure 2. Where the state-validation window opens when a check inspects mutable runtime state, and why binding to a tree hash removes it." /%}

## Measurements

Everything below comes from this repository between 28 July and 3 August 2026.
We pulled the numbers out of the git history, the GitHub API, and the published
chain. The monorepo is public, so you can recompute every one of them.

**Proof coverage.** 168 commits on `main` carry a `thwip-proof` trailer. Every
one of those envelopes decodes and parses against the shipped schema, all 168 of
them declaring `evaluationMode: head-tree`. Of the 132 merges, 128 carried a
proof on the pull request head. The 4 without one are pull requests #1, #2, #4
and #5, which merged before the App was enforcing.

**Suite time at the edge.** 504 suite results, all passing.

| Suite | Median | p90 | Max |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| lint | 0.3s | 0.9s | 3.7s |
| typecheck | 2.2s | 3.6s | 6.5s |
| test | 1.7s | 4.6s | 10.7s |
| **all three** | **4.5s** | **8.8s** | **16.0s** |

**Cost of reconstituting the environment.** Across 18 successful candidate
verifications in Actions, the job spends 5 seconds at the median on runner setup
and checkout, 14 seconds on install, and 17 seconds building workspace packages.
That is 36 seconds before any suite starts, and the suites then take 24 seconds
in that environment. The same three suites on a developer machine, with
dependencies and build outputs already present, take 12.5 seconds.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-setup-vs-work.svg" alt="Bar comparison of 36 seconds of environment rebuild against 4.5 seconds of suite execution at the edge" caption="Figure 3. On this repository, re-verifying in the cloud spends most of its time rebuilding a state the edge machine already holds." /%}

**A model, labelled as a model.** Suppose this repository had re-run the three
suites in Actions on every merge, once on the branch and again on the merge
group. At the 36 seconds of setup and 24 seconds of suites measured above, 132
merges would have consumed about 264 runner-minutes. GitHub's
[published rate](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/actions-minute-multipliers)
for a 2-core Linux runner is $0.006 per minute, in force since 1 January 2026,
which puts the bill at roughly $1.58.

We cannot put a billed figure beside that. GitHub's timing endpoint reports zero
billable milliseconds for every run on this repository, so run duration is all
we can measure: 599 minutes across 3,397 workflow runs. That number does not
compare with the model. 2,896 of those runs are the queue's own merge workflow,
which exists here because this repository is where thwip gets built.

At our size the dollar figure is trivial, so the ratio is the number to carry
across. Anybody whose repository takes 20 minutes to build can run the same
arithmetic and get something that matters.

**The chain.** `node scripts/verify-chain.mjs 1315419165` walks the published
merge chain from outside thwip, with no account and no token. When this was
written it reported 57 links, 57 of 57 signatures verified against the service
keys at `/.well-known/thwip-keys`, and no findings. Of those records, 47 are
`reused-proof-merge`, 5 are `candidate-verified-merge`, 4 are
`external-transition`, and 1 is a `base-correction`. Merging this post adds
another link, so run the script yourself for a current count. The chain only
reached that clean state on the evening of 3 August, after being broken since
the morning of 2 August.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-chain-kinds.svg" alt="Bar chart of 57 chain records by kind: 47 reused-proof merges, 5 candidate-verified merges, 4 external transitions, 1 base correction" caption="Figure 4. Every record on this repository's chain, by what it claims. The four external transitions are the merges of 2 August, described below." /%}

**Posture, published as it is.** Every one of the 168 proofs used the
`command` runner instead of a content-addressed
[Dagger](https://dagger.io/) pipeline, with `networkMode: unrestricted` and
`cacheMode: read-write`, which is the weakest posture the schema permits.
Behind them were 2 signers holding 3 device keys, working under 3 distinct
pipeline digests and policy versions 1, 3, and 4.

## The night the chain failed

We claim a signed chain is falsifiable by anybody, so on 3 August we pointed the
check at our own chain, and it came back with findings.

We wrote the script above for this post. Pointed at this repository, it returned
three `BASE_DISCONTINUITY` findings covering four pull requests that had merged
into `main` the day before with no record at all: #114, #117, #119 and #121.

The cause sits in the order of two operations. The queue signs a merge intent
that pins a candidate tree and the parents that tree hangs from, and then it
asks GitHub to merge. In between, the base moved. GitHub merged each head onto
the branch's real tip, producing a tree and a first parent that no intent had
pinned. Pull request 117's intent was signed sixteen seconds after its base had
already been superseded. Of the seventy merge intents in storage, zero pin any
of the four trees that landed, so there was no way to repair these as merges.

A second defect kept the queue quiet about all this. One of the merge writes
came back from GitHub as a 405, which GitHub returns transiently while it is
still computing mergeability. The workflow gave up, and the merge landed anyway.
Settling that write was the attempt's job, and we terminated and compacted the
attempt while the write was still open, so nothing ever read the row again.

Three changes stop this now. A merge write carries its own obligation, so a
scheduled sweep finds writes nobody resolved and asks git what became of them
([#142](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/pull/142)). Inside the guard that
signs, the authorize route reads the base from GitHub and defers the turn with
`BASE_MOVED` when the attempt has pinned a base the branch has already left
([#145](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/pull/145)). Whatever the route
decides, the reducer refuses a confirmation whose first parent differs from the
base the attempt pinned.

Two more pull requests came out of the same night.
[#143](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/pull/143) stopped spending a queue
operation on webhooks that carry no work, and
[#144](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/pull/144) made a chain finding
answerable from the project page. A fifth,
[#146](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/pull/146), closed the seam next door.
The merge workflow used to hand the queue the merged commit's tree and parents,
and the queue would sign a record repeating figures its own caller had chosen.
That endpoint now reads the commit from GitHub and refuses a commit GitHub will
not describe. All five merged through the queue with signed proofs. The queue
also runs the chain verifier against its own chain on every tick, so it will
report the next gap itself.

The chain carries an
[`external-transition`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/docs/heal.md)
for each of the four merges, saying that the base moved from one commit to
another and thwip did not certify the move. Those records name no pull request
and no policy, because neither would be true of them. A merge record would have
been the stronger claim available, and the evidence did not support one, so the
queue wrote down the weaker record instead.

## What this corpus cannot tell you

This corpus covers seven days, two signers and one repository, and we were
designing the system while it ran. What the measurements describe is what
happened here. They say nothing about how anybody else's repository would
behave. None of this is a controlled comparison, and the policy version kept
moving underneath the numbers while we collected them.

## What a proof does not buy

**A compromised signing device beats us.** An attacker holding an enrolled
private key can sign a well-formed proof over a tree whose suites never passed,
and the App will accept it. The App checks a signature and a subject, and it has
no view of the execution behind them, which is what discarding the trace costs
us.

What we offer against that is reproducibility and a public record. On any single
merge that is the weaker instrument, and an observer who watched the process
knows something we do not. Over a run of merges it starts paying, because every
proof binds to a content hash anybody can check out and re-run, and a signer
whose claims stop reproducing shows up in public in a way a private runtime
trace never would. It needs volume to work. A repository merging 131 times a
week builds up enough history to compare against.

The gap we cannot close is the first merge after a key compromise. Without
observing execution we have no mechanism for it, and it is listed under open
problems below.

The rest of the bounds:

- thwip never fetches your source. The App and the control plane verify
  metadata, digests, and signatures.
- A green check says one thing: approved suites passed on a tree, signed by an
  identified key. It is not a security review, and it says nothing about how the
  product behaves.
- Local and remote environments diverge. Work that needs the cloud as its
  execution environment should run there.
- Identity is GitHub's. Membership and repository permission come from the
  organisation, and we add no second invite graph.
- `fresh` and `audited` exist in the schema as names for assignment plus a
  signed integration receipt. Nothing in the system issues an assignment or
  signs a receipt today, so the CLI and the control plane refuse both by name
  instead of delivering `remote` under a stronger label. The `run.lease` field
  is already in the predicate for this.

## Check our chain

[`packages/queue-protocol`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/tree/main/packages/queue-protocol)
exports `verifyChain` and ships with its tests. Given the published records, it
checks that each link is the digest of the record before it, then resolves every
citation each record makes and reports any record carrying a gap where a
citation belongs.

[`scripts/verify-chain.mjs`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/scripts/verify-chain.mjs)
runs it against a live repository. It needs no thwip account:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/recallnet/thwip && cd thwip
pnpm install && pnpm build
node scripts/verify-chain.mjs recallnet/thwip
```

That script caught us, and the section above is its output. If you break our
chain with it, open an issue and paste what it printed, and we will act on it.

## Open problems

Ones we intend to close:

- Assignment and integration receipts, so `fresh` and `audited` mean something
  distinct from `remote`.
- Content-addressed pipelines as the default runner, so "same tree, same
  digests" comes out of the graph itself instead of resting on a convention.
- Hybrid matrices, where part of the suite has to stay remote for platform
  reasons.

Ones we do not know how to close:

- Detecting the first bad merge after a device compromise, without observing
  execution.
- Multi-party agreement on digests before a merge, at a cost a small team will
  pay.
- Whether `networkMode` and `cacheMode` can be made verifiable at the edge
  without reintroducing the sandbox literature's failure rates.

If you work on execution provenance and we have placed thwip wrongly in that
taxonomy, tell us and we will fix it. We would also like the citation for any
system that already does this and that we missed.

